      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "<strong>The Blue Jays finally remembered how to swing a bat</strong> - and it came at exactly the right moment, when the season was starting to feel like a slow drowning. After scoring just six runs across four games, Toronto erupted for 14 against Los Angeles on Saturday, riding a seven-run fifth inning fueled by Angels mistakes and Jays discipline. Ernie Clement went 5-for-5 with a homer. Brandon Valenzuela launched a three-run shot. It was the kind of offensive explosion this 18-21 roster has desperately needed, and it came with the team sitting 7.5 games back in a division where Tampa Bay and New York have already lapped the field. The win - Toronto's second straight - doesn't erase the math. Third place in the AL East isn't contention; it's treading water. But <strong>today's finale against the Angels at 12:37 p.m. ET offers a chance to build momentum before the schedule gets meaner</strong>. One night of fireworks doesn't save a season. Two straight might start to.",
        "updated": "Updated May 10, 2026"
      },
